<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithuanian Wars of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lithuanian Wars of Freedom · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lithuanian Wars of Independence were the three wars that newly independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> fought after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>: against Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army">West Russian Volunteer Army</a>, known as the Bermontians, and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Lithuania defeated the Soviet and Bermontian forces in 1919, but Poland kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> after Żeligowski's mutiny of 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanian-wars-of-independence.jpg" alt="Lithuanian Wars of Independence" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volunteers_of_the_Lithuanian_Armed_Forces_heading_to_the_Lithuanian_Wars_of_Independence_in_Vilkavi%C5%A1kis,_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> had been part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> since the partitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in 1795, and German forces occupied it from 1915. On 16 February 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-lithuania">Council of Lithuania</a> declared independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> acknowledged the declaration on 23 March 1918 but allowed no army, police or civil institutions. After the armistice of 11 November 1918 the first government, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustinas-voldemaras">Augustinas Voldemaras</a>, held that a small militia would be enough. The first law creating an army was passed on 23 November 1918, and the cabinet of Mykolas Sleževičius, formed on 26 December, called for volunteers, who were promised free land. Mobilisation began on 5 March 1919, and by the end of that summer the army numbered about 8,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> sought to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> as part of its revolution. The Lithuanian government left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> with the withdrawing German army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the city on 5 January 1919. German volunteers under Rüdiger von der Goltz helped Lithuanian forces halt the advance near Kėdainiai, and in February the allies won at Šėta and Jieznas, while Antanas Juozapavičius, the first Lithuanian officer to die in the wars, fell at Alytus. After General Silvestras Žukauskas took command on 7 May, a reorganised army took Utena, Panevėžys and Rokiškis, and by the end of August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> were defeated near Zarasai.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army">West Russian Volunteer Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov</a>, a mixed German-Russian force that aimed to keep German power in the region, occupied much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> and became notorious for looting. Lithuanian forces won near Radviliškis on 21 and 22 November 1919, and after the French General Henri Niessel intervened the Bermontians had left by 15 December. Lithuanian and Polish forces had clashed from spring 1919. On 8 October 1920, the day after the Suwałki Agreement was signed, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucjan-zeligowski">Lucjan Żeligowski</a> staged a mutiny arranged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. A Lithuanian counteroffensive in November, successful at Širvintos and Giedraičiai, was stopped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the peace treaty of 12 July 1920 Soviet Russia recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s independence and its right to the Vilnius Region. Poland did not recognise the treaty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> served as the temporary capital for 19 years, until Lithuanian troops entered Vilnius in October 1939 under the Soviet–Lithuanian Mutual Assistance Treaty. About 1,700 Lithuanian volunteers died in the fighting, though the historian Alfonsas Eidintas gives 1,444 deaths.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lithuanian Wars of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
